{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Evolution has no goal of knowledge growth, whereas Popperian science explicitly aims at it.","A tournament winner is often declared 'the best', whereas a corroborated theory is explicitly NOT declared 'more probable'."],"overturn_conditions":["If the 'severity' of a test cannot be defined without referencing the probability of the outcome.","If acting on a theory logically requires believing it will succeed, rendering the 'no probability' stance performatively contradictory."],"shared_structure":["Selection is based on survival of rigorous trials, not prediction of future dominance.","The 'winner' is simply the last one standing after attempts to eliminate it, not necessarily the 'best' in an absolute sense.","Preference is a strategy to keep the competition (testing) alive, not to end it with a final truth."],"source_memory_refs":["evolutionary_selection_pressure","championship_tournament_logic"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we bet on the undefeated boxer not because they are invincible, but because they have proven resilient against tough opponents, scientists prefer corroborated theories for their resilience, not their truth."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference of future success, but as a non-inductive methodological rule to maximize the 'content' and 'testability' of our current knowledge base, accepting that this choice carries no guarantee of truth or predictive reliability.","counterconditions":["If 'severity of test' implicitly assumes the uniformity of nature (that future conditions will resemble past testing conditions), then the preference smuggles in induction.","If the practical act of 'preferring' for action logically entails an expectation of future success, then the distinction between preference and probability collapses.","If the definition of 'better' relies on verisimilitude (truth-likeness) which Popper admitted cannot be formally assessed, then the criterion is empty."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument distinguishes between 'rational justification' (which implies probability/induction) and 'rational preference' (which implies selecting the most informative surviving conjecture). By defining corroboration as a report of past performance regarding test severity rather than a predictor of future performance, the scientist prefers the theory with the highest empirical content because it offers the most opportunities for future falsification. This preference is a strategic move within the game of science to accelerate error elimination, not a belief that the preferred theory is more likely to be true.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}